-- Fixed local game content. IDs stay stable so saved progress remains valid.
-- Re-running this seed adds missing rows without replacing existing rows.
BEGIN;

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO classic_levels (id, tier, title, passage) VALUES
    (1, 1, 'The Workshop Bell', 'At nine, the workshop bell rang across the courtyard. Mira set a box of loose screws beside the old clock and opened her notebook. She wrote down each small repair before touching a tool. By noon, the clock had a steady tick again, and the bell sounded less lonely in the quiet hall.'),
    (2, 1, 'A Library Map', 'The library kept a hand drawn map near its front desk. It showed narrow aisles, reading tables, and a tiny room for local history. Arun followed the map to a shelf he had never noticed. Between two atlases he found a book of simple machines, full of sketches that made each idea easy to test.'),
    (3, 1, 'Rain at the Stop', 'Rain began just as the bus reached the corner. People moved under the small roof and made room for a woman carrying a stack of paper. A child counted the drops sliding down the sign. When the bus arrived, everyone climbed aboard in order, and the driver waited until the last umbrella was folded.'),
    (4, 1, 'The Rooftop Garden', 'On the roof, ten clay pots stood in a neat row. Each held a different herb, with a paper label tied to the rim. Leena checked the soil with one finger before adding water. The mint needed a little more shade, so she moved it beside the wall. By evening, the leaves had lifted toward the cool air.'),
    (5, 1, 'Harbor Lights', 'At dusk, the harbor lamps came on one by one. A fishing boat returned slowly while two friends watched from the stone steps. They could hear ropes tap against the masts and gulls call over the water. The boat reached its place before dark, and a warm light appeared in the window of the little dock office.'),
    (6, 1, 'The Small Museum', 'The town museum had only three rooms, but every object had a story. Near the door stood a wooden radio that once carried news to the whole street. Nia read its label, then listened to a recording of its soft crackle. She left with a question for her grandfather about the songs he had heard when he was young.'),
    (7, 1, 'Morning Market', 'The market opened before sunrise. Sellers arranged baskets of oranges, fresh bread, and bright flowers along the lane. Dev brought a short list so he would not forget the rice. He stopped to help a vendor lift a heavy crate, then chose a small bunch of flowers for the kitchen table. The street was busy by the time he left.'),
    (8, 1, 'A Clear Night', 'The school telescope stood on a tripod in the field. Clouds had covered the sky all week, but tonight the stars were sharp. Sana adjusted the focus until the moon looked clear at the edge. She stepped aside so everyone could take a turn, then drew what she saw in a plain notebook under a small red lamp.'),
    (9, 1, 'The Bicycle Chain', 'A bicycle chain slipped off near the bridge. Omar turned the bike over, checked the gears, and put the chain back with careful hands. He wiped the oil from his fingers and gave the wheel a slow spin. It moved smoothly, so he packed his cloth and continued along the path before the afternoon heat grew strong.'),
    (10, 1, 'When the Lights Went Out', 'The lights went out during the family quiz. For a moment, everyone sat still and listened to the fan slow down. Then Ada found a lantern, and the game continued around the table. Without the television in the next room, they could hear each answer clearly. The power returned just as the final question was read.'),
    (11, 2, 'The Paper Bridge', 'A class built bridges from paper, tape, and string. Most groups made tall arches, but Rafi folded narrow strips into a low frame. He tested it with coins, adding one at a time until the center bent. After lunch, he changed the folds and tried again. The new bridge carried twice as many coins, although it looked almost the same. Rafi wrote the result beside his sketch so the next group could see what had changed.'),
    (12, 2, 'The Missing Recipe', 'Grandma kept her recipes in a blue tin above the stove. One morning, the card for lemon biscuits was missing. Tara searched the kitchen, checked the pantry, and even looked under the table. At last she found the card inside a library book, where Grandma had used it as a bookmark. The two baked together that afternoon. Tara copied the recipe into a fresh notebook and left the old card in its tin, safe from another trip to the library.'),
    (13, 2, 'Signals on the Hill', 'From the hill, the rescue team could see the entire valley. They placed three bright flags beside the trail to mark a safe route. A storm had washed away the usual signs, and hikers might miss the turn near the stream. Kiran checked each flag from the far ridge, then adjusted one that hid behind a tree. Before the team left, they walked the route again and drew its turns on a simple map for the visitor desk.'),
    (14, 2, 'A New Window', 'The art room had a window that faced a plain brick wall. The students asked if they could paint a small scene around its frame. First they drew a garden on paper, then they chose colors that would stay bright in winter. Their teacher measured the space and helped them mark the edges. When the painting was done, the real light made the green leaves glow. Even on cloudy days, the room felt a little more open.'),
    (15, 2, 'The Quiet Platform', 'At the station, Priya noticed a small board beside the timetable. Travelers had pinned notes there: a lost scarf, a found key, and a request for a ride to the village. She added a note about the blue umbrella she had seen on the last train. The next morning, someone had crossed out her note and written, ''Found it, thank you.'' Priya smiled at the answer. A busy platform could still feel like a place where people looked after one another.'),
    (16, 2, 'The Seed Exchange', 'Every spring, neighbors met at the community hall to trade seeds. Each packet carried a name, a planting date, and a short note about sunlight. Isha brought beans from her balcony garden and took home a few sunflower seeds. She planted them in two pots, one near the railing and one beside the door. A month later, the taller plant showed her which spot received more morning light. She wrote that lesson on next year''s packet.'),
    (17, 2, 'Repairing the Kite', 'The kite flew well until a sudden gust tore its paper tail. Manu caught it before it reached the pond and carried it home. He found light cloth, cut a new tail, and tied it with two firm knots. The first test failed because the cloth was too heavy. He shortened it, moved the knot, and tried again in the open field. This time the kite rose smoothly above the trees, and Manu watched the tail follow each turn without a wobble.'),
    (18, 2, 'Notes from the Ferry', 'On the morning ferry, a student counted the birds that followed the boat. She marked each kind in a pocket notebook and noted where it appeared. Near the shore, she saw many small birds; farther out, only two large ones circled overhead. The captain explained that wind and food both changed their routes. By the time the ferry reached the other bank, her page was full of marks and questions for the science club.'),
    (19, 2, 'The Old Projector', 'The school found an old projector in a storage cupboard. Its lamp still worked, but the picture was dim and crooked. Ezra cleaned the lens with a soft cloth while his teacher checked the stand. They moved the screen closer and adjusted the focus one small turn at a time. Soon a sharp map appeared on the wall. The class used it to trace a river from the mountains to the sea, cheering when the projector held steady through the lesson.'),
    (20, 2, 'A Bench for Everyone', 'The park had a long path but only one bench, close to the gate. A group of neighbors planned another rest spot halfway around the lake. They asked walkers where a bench would help most, measured a level patch, and sent their idea to the park office. When the new bench arrived, they planted flowers nearby. An older runner stopped there on opening day and said the extra place to rest made the whole loop feel welcoming.'),
    (21, 3, 'The Clockmaker''s Ledger', 'In the back of the shop, a clockmaker kept a ledger for every repair. Each entry listed the date, the fault, the part replaced, and the sound of the clock after testing. When a customer returned with a familiar brass clock, the ledger showed that its spring had been changed years before. The new fault was elsewhere: a grain of dust had lodged beside a gear. The clockmaker cleaned it, waited through two complete turns, and recorded the quiet tick. The old note had saved him from replacing a part that still worked perfectly.'),
    (22, 3, 'A Route Through Fog', 'Fog settled over the valley before the walking group reached the ridge. Their guide stopped at a marked post and checked the paper map against a compass. A shortcut across open ground looked tempting, but the group stayed on the visible trail. They counted posts aloud, kept the last walker in sight, and paused whenever the path divided. By late morning the fog thinned enough to reveal the bridge below. The slower route had added twenty minutes, yet everyone arrived together with dry shoes and a clear record of each turn.'),
    (23, 3, 'The Community Archive', 'The archive stored letters, photographs, and recordings donated by local families. Each item needed a date and a brief description before it could be placed in a box. While sorting one folder, Amita noticed two photos of the same street taken forty years apart. The bakery had become a clinic, but the stone fountain remained. She labeled both images carefully and invited their owners to confirm the dates. Without those details, a visitor might mistake a memory for a fact, and the archive would be less useful to everyone.'),
    (24, 3, 'Testing the Water Wheel', 'A small water wheel stood beside the science lab sink. Four students wanted to learn whether wider blades would turn it faster. They marked a fixed line on the tap, timed ten rotations, and wrote each result in a table. Then they changed only the blades and repeated the test three times. One trial was unusually slow because a blade touched the frame. They noted the problem instead of hiding it. Their final chart showed a modest gain, not a miracle, and their teacher praised the honest measurements.'),
    (25, 3, 'Letters Across Town', 'When the town post office moved, some residents still left letters at the old building. The new clerk placed a clear notice on its door, but he also visited nearby shops to explain the change. For two weeks, a volunteer checked the empty box each evening and carried any stray mail to the new desk. A birthday card arrived late but reached its reader before the party. The clerk kept a list of common mistakes, then used it to make the directions on the notice shorter and easier to follow for every visitor.'),
    (26, 3, 'The Lantern Parade', 'The neighborhood lantern parade was planned for a narrow lane, so safety mattered as much as color. Volunteers tested each battery light, marked the uneven stones, and left space for people walking slowly. Children built paper lanterns shaped like birds and boats, while parents tied the handles firmly. At dusk the group moved at a gentle pace, stopping twice so the line could close up. From an upstairs window, the lanterns looked like a bright stream. By the final corner, nobody had been left behind.'),
    (27, 3, 'A Fair Trade of Tools', 'Four families shared a shed of tools instead of buying the same equipment many times. A notebook near the door recorded who borrowed each item and when it would return. One Saturday, a drill was missing just as Neel needed it to fix a loose shelf. The record showed that a neighbor had borrowed it, so Neel called and arranged to collect it after lunch. They agreed to add a simple return box outside the shed. The system was not perfect, but a small change made it easier for everyone to keep track.'),
    (28, 3, 'The Theater Rehearsal', 'During rehearsal, the stage lights dimmed before the final scene. The actors stopped, and the director checked the cue list instead of guessing. A lighting note had been written beside the wrong line, two pages early. The team moved it, ran the scene again, and watched for any other missed cues. Asha then read each instruction aloud while another student marked it on the script. By opening night, the changes were small enough that the audience noticed only the story, not the careful work behind it.'),
    (29, 3, 'A Field of Wind Vanes', 'The weather club placed five wind vanes around the school grounds. One stood near a wall, another by the field, and the rest sat among trees. At noon, their arrows did not all point the same way. The students checked that each vane could turn freely, then compared the readings with notes about nearby obstacles. The wall had sheltered one vane from the breeze. Rather than calling the instruments broken, the club moved it to an open spot and wrote down why its first readings had differed from the others.'),
    (30, 3, 'The Book Cart Schedule', 'A mobile book cart visited three streets on Fridays, but its timetable confused new readers. The printed sheet listed stops by name without giving times or landmarks. Farah rode with the cart, measured how long each stop took, and drew a clearer route. She added a note about the market crossing, where traffic sometimes caused a delay. The librarian tested the revised sheet with two families who had never used the service. Both found the cart on their first try, and the schedule stayed beside its window.'),
    (31, 4, 'Inventory at the Observatory', 'Before the observatory opened for the season, the volunteers counted every lens, chart, and spare battery. Their inventory from last year listed eight red lamps, but only seven were on the shelf. Leela checked the sign-out book and found that one lamp had gone to a school visit in March. A teacher confirmed it was still in a classroom cupboard and promised to return it. The team did not mark the lamp as lost; they recorded its location and the date of the call. Later, when visitors arrived for a night of viewing, the small record kept the staff from searching again. A careful inventory had turned a mystery into an ordinary item on a list.'),
    (32, 4, 'The Narrow Canal', 'The canal carried water to two neighborhoods, but a fallen branch had slowed the current near the old mill. Residents from both sides met to decide how to clear it without damaging the stone bank. One group wanted to pull the branch at once; another suggested waiting for a dry morning and marking the weak stones first. They chose the slower plan. At sunrise, they set ropes beyond the fragile edge, lifted the branch in sections, and checked the flow before leaving. Water reached both streets again by noon. The meeting had taken longer than the work, yet it prevented an avoidable repair to the canal wall.'),
    (33, 4, 'A Puzzle of Street Names', 'An old map called the lane Orchard Road, while newer signs named it Market Lane. For a local history project, Samir searched council records, interviewed shopkeepers, and compared photographs of the corner. He discovered that a market replaced the orchard after a flood, but the name changed much later. One photograph even showed both names on separate signs. In his report, he kept the dates in order and marked an uncertain year with a question rather than inventing an answer. The class used his timeline to explain how a place can change twice: first in its daily use, then in the words people choose for it.'),
    (34, 4, 'The Glasshouse Thermometer', 'Inside the glasshouse, seedlings wilted each afternoon although the soil stayed damp. Meera suspected heat, so she placed thermometers at three heights and wrote down the readings every hour. The warmest air collected near the roof, but the table beside the south window also became unusually hot. She opened a roof vent and moved the most delicate plants away from that glass. Over the next week, the leaves stayed firm and the new measurements dropped slightly. The lesson was not that one number explained every problem. It was that several careful readings helped her choose a change she could actually test.'),
    (35, 4, 'The Exhibition Label', 'A museum planned to display a cracked clay bowl found near the river. The first label called it a cooking pot, although no one had proved what it held. Kavya asked the team to examine the notes from the excavation: the bowl had traces of ash nearby, but none inside it. They changed the label to describe what was known and added a sentence about possible uses. A visitor could now see the object and the uncertainty together. At the opening, a child asked why the bowl had a crack. The guide answered honestly that it might have broken in use or much later underground, and invited the child to study its edges.'),
    (36, 4, 'Emergency Drill at the Hall', 'The community hall ran an evacuation drill before its annual fair. Volunteers mapped two exits, placed a clear sign near the back door, and checked that the ramp remained free of boxes. During the practice, a group followed the main hallway and met a delivery cart blocking half the space. The leader stopped, moved the group to the other exit, and noted the obstacle afterward. The fair organizers shifted the delivery schedule so carts would not arrive while the hall was crowded. The drill did not make anyone look perfect. It revealed a simple problem while there was still time to fix it, which was exactly why the practice mattered.'),
    (37, 4, 'A Letter with Two Dates', 'While digitizing family letters, Noor found a page dated 12 April on the front and 13 April on the back. She first assumed one date was wrong, then noticed that the writer had continued after midnight. The ink was darker on the second side, and the greeting had changed from ''dear brother'' to a short note about the morning train. Noor scanned both sides in order and added an explanation to the catalog. She did not erase either date to make the file look tidy. Keeping the small inconsistency gave future readers a better picture of how the letter was written: slowly, across an ordinary night, by someone waiting for news.'),
    (38, 4, 'The Second Estimate', 'The school needed a new shade over its courtyard tables. Two builders gave estimates, but the cheaper one did not include the cost of strong anchors for the posts. A student team compared both lists line by line and asked each builder the same questions about materials, weather, and repairs. After the missing items were added, the totals were close. The school chose the design that could be repaired with common parts, even though its first price was slightly higher. Months later, a loose panel took one afternoon to replace. The students learned that a useful estimate shows what is included, not merely a number at the bottom.'),
    (39, 4, 'The Orchard Survey', 'A village group surveyed an old orchard before planting new trees. They counted living trunks, measured shade at midday, and asked residents which paths they actually used. One map showed a straight walkway through the center, but footmarks curved around a low patch that flooded after rain. The group adjusted its plan to follow the dry ground instead of forcing people onto the old line. They planted young trees away from the busiest path and kept space for a bench near the entrance. The final drawing looked less symmetrical than the first, yet it matched the place and the people who walked through it.'),
    (40, 4, 'A Signal in the Workshop', 'In a noisy workshop, spoken warnings could vanish beneath the sound of machines. The team agreed on three simple hand signals: stop, step back, and need help. They practiced while every machine was off, then tested the signals during a short supervised task. One gesture looked too much like an ordinary wave, so they replaced it before anyone relied on it. A printed card near each bench showed the final set. During a later drill, a student spotted a loose cable and signaled stop across the room. The machines paused, the cable was secured, and nobody had to guess what the raised hand meant that day.'),
    (41, 5, 'The Missing Hour in the Log', 'At the weather station, the daily log jumped from 2:00 to 3:00 on a quiet Sunday. Nikhil thought an hour of readings had vanished, but the sensor file showed a steady sequence of measurements. He checked the wall clock, the station computer, and the paper instructions left by the previous team. The wall clock had been moved forward for a seasonal time change, while the sensor recorded time without that adjustment. Nikhil added a note beside the gap and kept the original entries unchanged. When he drew the weekly chart, he used the sensor times and labeled the axis clearly for the whole team. A missing hour on one clock was not missing data; treating it that way would have spoiled a useful record.'),
    (42, 5, 'A Trial for the New Bridge', 'The footbridge across Willow Creek opened only after a careful trial. Engineers placed measured weights at several points and watched how the deck moved, while volunteers kept the path clear. A small bend appeared near the center, exactly where the design predicted it would. Still, the team checked the bolts, the rail height, and the steps at both ends before inviting walkers across. The first visitors were a pair of neighbors carrying groceries; they paused halfway to look at the water below. Later, the project group posted a short report that explained both the successful checks and one railing adjustment. The bridge felt simple to cross because many small decisions had been made in the right order.'),
    (43, 5, 'Three Versions of a Story', 'For the oral history exhibit, Rosa recorded three people describing the same winter storm. One remembered the school closing before noon, another recalled walking home at dusk, and the third insisted that classes continued the next day. Rather than selecting a favorite account, Rosa checked old notices and wrote a timeline beside the recordings. The notices confirmed a half-day closure, but they could not explain every memory. She left the voices intact and described where the evidence agreed or differed. Visitors heard not only what happened, but how a busy day can look different from each window. The exhibit became more trustworthy when it made room for uncertainty instead of pretending the accounts matched perfectly.'),
    (44, 5, 'The Ferry''s Spare Part', 'A ferry mechanic found that a small valve had begun to stick during morning checks. The boat could still move, but the crew delayed departure until they understood the fault. Their spare valve looked identical from a distance; its label, however, listed a different pressure rating. The mechanic compared the manual, measured the old fitting, and called the supplier before installing anything. A proper replacement arrived by road that afternoon. Passengers were frustrated by the delay, yet the captain explained the choice plainly and arranged another crossing. The crew recorded the mismatch in its parts list so the wrong spare would not be selected next month. A few careful minutes at the shelf had prevented a much larger problem on the water.'),
    (45, 5, 'A Debate Over the Square', 'The town council proposed turning an empty square into a parking area. Shop owners hoped for easier deliveries, while families wanted space for trees and evening games. At a public meeting, a student group brought counts of vehicles, pedestrians, and bicycle trips at different hours. Their numbers showed that delivery traffic peaked early, long before children used the square. The council tested a compromise: a loading lane open until noon, with movable planters protecting the rest of the space. After a month, the group repeated its counts and collected comments from both sides. The trial did not satisfy every wish, but it gave the council evidence to revise the plan instead of guessing who used the square most.'),
    (46, 5, 'The Archive''s Duplicate Box', 'Two boxes in the archive carried the same label, ''River Survey, 1984,'' yet their contents were not identical. One held field notes and rough maps; the other contained clean drawings prepared months later. If a volunteer had merged them without checking, the order of the work would have disappeared. Zoya compared dates, matched page numbers, and gave each box a fuller description. She placed a cross-reference in the catalog so readers could find both versions. A visiting researcher later used the rough map to understand why a bend in the river was redrawn. The difference between a draft and a final copy had seemed minor on the shelf, but it mattered deeply to the story the records could tell.'),
    (47, 5, 'A Long Night at the Shelter', 'When heavy rain closed the hill road, the community shelter opened before midnight. Volunteers divided tasks: one registered arrivals, two prepared tea, and another checked blankets against a simple list. A family arrived without medicine that had been left at home, so the coordinator contacted the local clinic and documented the request. No one promised a solution before the clinic replied. By dawn, the road was still closed, but everyone had a place to rest and knew when the next update would come. Afterward, the team reviewed its notes and discovered that clear signs near the entrance would have reduced confusion. They added those signs before the next storm season, treating the night as a lesson rather than a victory speech.'),
    (48, 5, 'The Printer''s Color Proof', 'A print shop prepared posters for the spring festival. On the screen, the orange border looked warm; on the first paper proof, it appeared almost red. The designer did not simply change every color at once. She checked the paper stock, printed a small test strip, and compared it under daylight and the shop lamps. The problem came from the paper''s yellow tint, which shifted several colors unevenly. She chose a cooler orange and asked the festival team to approve a second proof. The final poster matched the intended mood without hiding the difference between screen and paper. In her notes, she saved both tests so the next project on that stock would begin with evidence rather than another round of guesses.'),
    (49, 5, 'The Orchard''s Shared Ledger', 'Three families cared for a shared orchard, and each kept a different account of the harvest. One counted baskets, one counted fruit, and the third recorded only what was sold. At the end of summer their totals seemed to disagree badly. Instead of accusing anyone of a mistake, they agreed on common units and copied the original notes into one ledger. They separated fruit eaten at home, fruit donated, and fruit taken to market. A few missing dates remained uncertain, so they marked those rows rather than filling them from memory. By autumn the ledger gave a clearer picture of the orchard''s yield. It also showed which trees needed more care, a question none of the earlier lists had been designed to answer.'),
    (50, 5, 'The Final Route Check', 'Before a cycling event, organizers walked the full route with a map and a checklist. They verified each turn, noted rough pavement, and placed water points where riders could stop without blocking traffic. At the third junction, a sign was visible from one approach but hidden by leaves from the other. The team moved it, then returned on bicycles to test the route at riding speed. They also checked that volunteers knew whom to call if a participant needed help. On event morning, a sudden shower changed the road surface, so the first group started slowly and reported conditions to those behind. The route had been planned carefully, yet the organizers still watched it as it changed. Good preparation gave them room to respond instead of pretending every detail was fixed forever.');

